            <ArticleTitle>A mindful eating group intervention for obese women: a mixed methods feasibility study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>211-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.apnu.2013.05.004</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0883-9417(13)00072-1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The purpose of this mixed methods study was to: 1) describe the effect of an 8-week mindful eating intervention on mindful eating, weight loss self-efficacy, depression, and biomarkers of weight in urban, underserved, obese women; and 2) identify themes of the lived experience of mindful eating. A convenience sample of 12 obese women was recruited with data collected at baseline and 8 weeks followed by a focus group. Only self-efficacy for weight loss significantly increased over 8 weeks (t=-2.63, P=.04). Qualitative findings of mindful eating supported quantitative findings and extended understanding about the effect of the intervention.</AbstractText>
